[Music]  no matter how busy with the manny the  middle aspect always so much harder and  when you're swimming six to ten hours by  yourself  i started running out of things to think  about  your mind starts getting exhausted  my name is chris frame and i swam the  entire length of the mississippi river  it took me 181 days  [Music]  the mississippi river is 2  320 miles long  beginning in minnesota it empties into  the gulf of mexico  and in 2015 chris rang took it on to  raise awareness and honor gold star  families  the gold star family is a family that's  lost a loved one in service to our  country which is truly inspiring so i  was like i want to do  something  so he took on the mighty mississippi  it's an amazing powerful river so you  don't really know what to expect  we did as much research as possible in  what you'll face in the mississippi  you see a lot of wildlife out there  you'll see bald eagles flying around a  lot of alligators stuff like that  one thing that i was really kind of  surprised about was how big the swells  get in the river you know i didn't  expect 10 plus foot swells  the only time that i would not swim  was when there was a fog so if there's  storms still swimming through the storms  you know we did hit some pretty big  storms going down the river one storm he  remembers in particular we're making  good time and before you know it sky is  just pitch black it's just sideways rain  winds changing constant direction the  lightning's flashing around and  you can feel it it's hitting so closely  actually you can feel it  in the water  and there's nothing really can do at  that point you know it's you're waiting  out  i've always had a high respect and  appreciation for nature  there's days that it took me an hour  just to go half a mile  just seeing how quickly that river can  change not only from weather but you  know from currents  you know so a new appreciation for what  that river itself for what can come from  what you'll face in the mississippi  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  be  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  participate  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you know it's so funny i get this  question all the time do you still  remember how it felt to drown  it was a crippling feeling i was  suffocating underwater now i've made it  my job to conquer my fear and to help to  get these kids swim lessons and learn  how to respect the water  [Music]  my name is colin jones i was in the  olympics in 2008 and 2012 and i love to  give swim lessons cullen jones is a  four-time olympic medalist swimmer for  team usa right after getting the gold  medal in 2008 they put the drowning  rates in front of me  and i learned that 70 of african  americans nearly 60 of latin americans  nearly 40 percent of caucasians don't  notice them so it's a big problem all  over the us  i think the reason that you don't see as  many african americans in swimming is  because there's such a stigma that  black people don't swim and this is  something that needs to be broken  because we are losing children you're  losing adults and it's  something that can be changed by swim  lessons  swimming lessons are so important to  kids to me personally because i was  almost one of those kids and part of  this statistic  when cullen was just five years old he  almost drowned on a water slide at an  amusement park they had to come out and  grab me and resuscitate me because i was  under water for about 30 seconds and i  think what's important about the story  is that i was fully supervised i mean it  was that easy and that quick for me to  go underwater that could have been my  last day on this earth  and with that near-death experience  colin has made it his platform to bring  water safety education to kids from all  backgrounds  the way that i'm trying to change my  community is definitely with working  with the make splash initiative i knew  this was my way of giving back to a  sport that's given me so much  once you learn how to swim it's like  riding a bike you never forget  so take the time learn how to swim  [Music]  when you're looking out from the  platform  usually  it's just water everywhere which makes  you feel really really tiny jumping into  this  giant ocean  i'm rachelle simpson  and i'm a world champion cliff driver  [Applause]  it's very similar to olympic diving the  only difference is at the end we're  going to land on our feet  as i climb up on the platform you hear  the wind blowing in your ears  sometimes there's seagulls zooming  around my head  i hear the announcer call my name  it's kind of like i'm going into this  hallway and the sound slowly cutting out  because that's when i'm going into focus  that's when i set my feet and then my  arms go up  i count to three  and go  your body's on autopilot and your brain  is just sitting back watching front  somersault twist second somersault tight  position  [Music]  when you hit the water at first you  don't notice anything and then it starts  all coming back you create bubbles  underneath the surface and you feel  those bubbles pushing you back up to the  surface quite a cool feeling actually  [Music]  when you come up take a breath of air  and the rush of adrenaline just like  you've been holding it off  and not even breathing until you hit  that water  [Applause]  each time i enter the water it's another  successful moment of convincing my brain  to do something i'm scared of but each  time i get a sense of empowerment when i  overcome it  i love it  [Applause]  [Music]  on eli malk island in palau  there is a lake like no other  [Music]  the saltwater lake was once connected to  the ocean but as the island changed it  was closed off  now in isolation it is filled with a  jellyfish species found nowhere else  the golden jellyfish  these cryptic creatures spend their days  migrating across the lake from one side  to the other following the path of the  sun which feeds the fast-growing algae  that this jellyfish species feeds on  and with no predators to keep them in  check the jellyfish swarm and fill the  lake like a cloudy dream  it's safe for humans to swim in because  though these jellyfish do have stingers  they are too small to be felt by humans  gliding through this place feels  otherworldly  [Music]  you

